I'm going to write the prompt now.
Write a self-contained prompt that instructs an AI coding agent to build a
Python 3 command-line script that scans a set of directories for Git
repositories and reports which of them have uncommitted changes.
Follow the specification below exactly; do not include source code or code
snippets in your output. **Purpose:** The script checks the working-tree status
of multiple local Git repositories in parallel and prints a concise report, so
the user can see at a glance which repositories have uncommitted or untracked
work. **Target environment:** The script runs under Termux on Android.
Its shebang line points to the Termux Python interpreter path
(`/data/data/com.termux/files/usr/bin/env python`).
Use only the Python standard library.
Use the `from __future__ import annotations` import so modern type hints (such
as `X | None` and `tuple[...]`) work. **Dependencies:** Standard library only:
`subprocess`, `sys`, `multiprocessing` (`Pool`, `cpu_count`), and
`pathlib.Path`.
The script requires the `git` executable to be available on the system PATH.
**Per-repository check (a worker function):** - Accept a `Path` to a repository
directory and return a tuple of `(repo_path, has_changes: bool, message: str |
None)`. - First verify that `<repo_path>/.git` exists and is a directory.
If not, return `(repo_path, False, "Not a git repository")`. - Otherwise run
`git status --porcelain` with the repository as the working directory, capturing
stdout and stderr as text, with a timeout of 5 seconds. - If the command exits
with a non-zero return code, return `(repo_path, False, "Git error: <stderr
stripped>")`. - If stdout is non-empty (after stripping), it indicates changes.
Split the output by newlines.
For each line, take the first two characters as the status code and the text
from index 3 onward as the file path.
Skip entries whose status code is blank after stripping whitespace.
Format each entry as `"<status> <file>"`. - Join at most the first 5 entries
with `", "`.
If there are more than 5 entries, append `" (+N more)"` where N is the remaining
count.
Return `(repo_path, True, summary)`. - If stdout is empty, return `(repo_path,
False, "Clean")`. - Catch `subprocess.TimeoutExpired` and return `(repo_path,
False, "Timeout checking git status")`. - Catch any other exception and return
`(repo_path, False, "<error message>")` using the exception's string form, so
one failing repository never crashes the whole run. **Main program flow:** -
Determine the list of repository paths to scan from the command-line arguments
(directories passed as arguments).
Each argument is a path to a repository; convert each to a `Path`.
Provide sensible handling if no arguments are given (print a short usage message
to stderr and exit with a non-zero status), and decide whether to also accept a
parent directory and scan its immediate subdirectories — document the chosen
behavior in a usage string. - Run the worker across all repositories in parallel
using `multiprocessing.Pool` sized to `cpu_count()`, using `pool.map` (or
equivalent) so results are returned in input order. - Print a report: a header
line, then one line per repository showing a marker (for example a checkmark or
a clear symbol for clean, and a warning symbol for changes or errors), the
repository's name (directory name), and the message.
Make the output readable in a plain terminal. - At the end, print a summary line
with counts of repositories with changes, clean repositories, and
non-repositories or errors. - Exit with status 0 when all repositories are
clean, and a non-zero status (e.g., 1) when any repository has uncommitted
changes, so the script is usable in shell scripts.
Print nothing extra to stdout beyond the report. **Execution details:** - Guard
the entry point with `if __name__ == "__main__":` so multiprocessing works
correctly (required for spawn-based start methods). - Make the script executable
with the Termux shebang line at the top. **Edge cases to handle:** - A path that
does not exist or is not a directory: treat as "Not a git repository" (the
`.git` check covers this) without crashing. - Repositories with a `.git` file
instead of directory (e.g., worktrees/submodules): the spec treats these as not
a repository; mention this as a known limitation in a code comment. - Very long
file lists: truncate to 5 entries as described. - Paths with spaces: pass the
path as a `Path` object, not a shell string. - Command output with trailing
whitespace or blank lines: strip before splitting. **Style:** Keep the code
short, typed with the same annotations as described, use concise docstrings or
comments only where helpful, and avoid external libraries. ---
